}

::CW_U05_L02_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Draft: The rain stopped. I found the key. The porch was empty. Which revision improves cohesion?{
~A. The rain stopped. I found the key. The porch was empty.#Incorrect. This repeats the choppy original. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Key rain porch stopped found.#Incorrect. This is not clear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The porch was a key and rain.#Incorrect. This changes meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. After the rain stopped, I found the key on the empty porch.#Correct. This connects the ideas smoothly without changing the meaning.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Draft: i waited by the window it was dark Which revision best fixes conventions?{
=A. I waited by the window. It was dark.#Correct. This fixes capitalization and separates the run-on into clear sentences.
~B. i waited by the window it was dark.#Incorrect. Capitalization and sentence boundary errors remain. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I waited by the window it was dark.for this item#Incorrect. This still needs clearer punctuation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. waited I dark window by.#Incorrect. Word order is unclear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q04::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Which revision improves clarity? Original: They put it there after it happened.{
~A. They did it there.#Incorrect. This remains vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It was a thing.#Incorrect. This is unclear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. After the storm, Mia put the flashlight on the kitchen table.#Correct. This replaces vague pronouns with specific people, objects, and places.
~D. Something happened somewhere.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q05::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Which sentence best explains cohesion?{
~A. Cohesion means spelling every word aloud.#Incorrect. That is not cohesion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Cohesion means deleting every transition.#Incorrect. Transitions often support cohesion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Cohesion means writing without paragraphs forever.#Incorrect. Paragraphs can help organization. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Cohesion means ideas connect smoothly so readers can follow the writing.#Correct. Cohesion is about how sentences and ideas fit together.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q06::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Draft: I opened the envelope there was nothing inside. What convention issue should be fixed?{
~A. Incorrect capitalization of I.#Incorrect. I is capitalized. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The sentence needs punctuation or separation between two complete thoughts.#Correct. The sentence is a run-on and needs a boundary.
~C. Misspelled envelope.#Incorrect. Envelope is spelled correctly. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Missing character name only.#Incorrect. The main convention issue is sentence boundary.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q07::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Which revision keeps creative voice while improving conventions? Original: no sound. just the clock.{
=A. No sound. Just the clock.#Correct. This keeps the fragment style while controlling capitalization and punctuation.
~B. There was no auditory experience except a timekeeping device.#Incorrect. This flattens the creative voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. no sound just clock no punctuation.#Incorrect. This remains uncontrolled. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Delete the line.#Incorrect. The line can be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q08::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Draft: The scene jumps from the character leaving school to arriving home with no signal. What should the writer add?{
