}

::CW_U05_L02_Q12::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Draft: The lamp flickered. The room was cold. I heard footsteps. Which revision improves cohesion without changing meaning?{
~A. The lamp was a sandwich.#Incorrect. This changes meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I heard footsteps and deleted the lamp.#Incorrect. It removes a detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Cold cold cold lamp.#Incorrect. This is unclear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The lamp flickered, and the cold room went still when I heard footsteps.#Correct. This connects the details smoothly while preserving the original meaning.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q13::[MLA.CW.REV.02] Why should convention edits usually come after larger revision?{
~A. Because conventions never matter.#Incorrect. They do matter. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Because spelling is impossible to fix.#Incorrect. Spelling can be fixed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Larger revision may change sentences, so final convention edits should happen after the main ideas are stable.#Correct. Editing too early can waste effort if the sentence changes later.
~D. Because readers prefer errors.#Incorrect. Readers need control. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

